        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe structure is clean, additive, and idiomatic for this codebase:\n\n- **`householdSize` on `Owner` mirrors the existing `namesakeCount` pattern** \u2014 a denormalized, persisted counter that feeds a derived getter. The field, column, and accessor placement all sit alongside their established neighbors, with schema changes applied consistently across all four DB dialects.\n- **`getMembershipTier()` remains the single place that derives the tier.** The GOLD branch is layered on top as the highest priority, with the prior SILVER/BRONZE logic left intact below it \u2014 the method stays cohesive rather than becoming a catch-all, and the Javadoc was updated to match.\n- **Stamping `householdSize` belongs in `joinHousehold()`**, which already owns household identity assignment. Computing `members.size() + 1` and writing it to every member plus the new owner is the natural home for this, reading the count \"back from any of them\" exactly as the household-id comment already describes.\n- **The loop simplification is appropriate**: the old `householdId`-equality guard only made sense when the id was the sole thing being written; now that every member's size changes on each create, unconditionally setting both fields and saving is the correct and simpler shape.\n\nA maintainer familiar with the existing `namesakeCount`/`householdId` machinery would find this a natural extension."
